{
  "id": "80722",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "Radiometric Dating",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Several radioisotopes have half-lives and other properties that make them useful for the purposes of determining the approximate age and origin of objects, such as archaeological artifacts, formerly living organisms, or geological formations. This process, "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "radiometric dating"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A technique used to determine the age of materials such as rocks or carbon by measuring the relative proportions of specific radioactive isotopes and their decay products. This method relies on the known half-lives of these isotopes to calculate the time elapsed since the material was formed."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2740061167",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2116253839",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ", is responsible for many breakthrough scientific discoveries about the geological history of the earth, the evolution of life, and the history of human civilization. We explore some of the most common types of radioactive dating and how the particular isotopes work for each type. "
              }
            ],
            "id": "w0ru3xt9xdlialt",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "yeekuqbaf2bhchh",
                "text": "Radioactive Dating Using Carbon"
              }
            ],
            "id": "8jd6xo98h5v7yp8",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The radioactivity of carbon-14 provides a method for dating objects that were a part of a living organism. This method of radiometric dating, which is also called "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "radiocarbon dating"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A method for dating objects up to about 50,000 years old that were derived from once-living matter. It measures the ratio of carbon-14 (¹⁴C) to carbon-12 (¹²C) in the object and compares it to the ratio in the present-day atmosphere. This technique relies on the known half-life of ¹⁴C to calculate the time since the organism's death."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "3886465108",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1819271051",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " or carbon-14 dating, is accurate for dating carbon-containing substances that are up to about 30,000 years old, and it can provide reasonably accurate dates up to a maximum of about 50,000 years old."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2w6gzciig7frx83",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Naturally occurring carbon consists of three isotopes: "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_6^{12}\\mathrm C",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": ", which constitutes about 99% of the carbon on earth; "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_6^{13}\\mathrm C",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": ", about 1% of the total; and trace amounts of "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_6^{14}\\mathrm C",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": ". Carbon-14 forms in the upper atmosphere by the reaction of nitrogen atoms with neutrons from cosmic rays in space:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "ugn8v0z14irx428",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "ysog756py3i7tvn",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "fb3wgucvv83ahr0",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "{}_7^{14}\\mathrm N\\;+\\;{}_0^1\\mathrm n\\;\\rightarrow\\;{}_6^{14}\\mathrm C\\;+\\;{}_1^1\\mathrm H",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "All isotopes of carbon react with oxygen to produce CO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": " molecules. The ratio of "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_6^{14}\\mathrm{CO}_2",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " to "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_6^{12}\\mathrm{CO}_2",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " depends on the ratio of "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_6^{14}\\mathrm{CO}",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " to "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_6^{12}\\mathrm{CO}",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " in the atmosphere. The natural abundance of "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_6^{14}\\mathrm{CO}",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " in the atmosphere is approximately 1 part per trillion; until recently, this has generally been constant over time, as seen in gas samples found trapped in ice. The incorporation of "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_6^{14}\\mathrm{CO}_2",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " and "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_6^{12}\\mathrm{CO}_2",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " into plants is a regular part of the photosynthesis process, which means that the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_6^{14}\\mathrm C:{}_6^{12}\\mathrm C",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " ratio found in a living plant is the same as the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_6^{14}\\mathrm C:{}_6^{12}\\mathrm C",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " ratio in the atmosphere. But when the plant dies, it no longer traps carbon through photosynthesis. Because "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_6^{12}\\mathrm C",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " is a stable isotope and does not undergo radioactive decay, its concentration in the plant does not change. However, carbon-14 decays by "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "\\beta",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " emission with a half-life of 5730 years:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "rqyit9hgjflo114",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "noz2e1f4c5jjmu0",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "1v0n34x7z14z8cj",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "{}_6^{14}\\mathrm C\\;\\rightarrow\\;{}_7^{14}\\mathrm N\\;+{}_{-1}^0\\mathrm e",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Thus, the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_6^{14}\\mathrm C:{}_6^{12}\\mathrm C",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " ratio gradually decreases after the plant dies. The decrease in the ratio with time provides a measure of the time that has elapsed since the death of the plant (or other organism that ate the plant). The image below visually depicts this process."
              }
            ],
            "id": "6c6nw3iawrtnwxv",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "alt": "A diagram shows a cow standing on the ground next to a tree. In the upper left of the diagram, where the sky is represented, a single white sphere is shown and is connected by a downward-facing arrow to a larger sphere composed of green and white spheres that is labeled “superscript 14, subscript 7, N.” This structure is connected to three other structures by a right-facing arrow. Each of the three it points to are composed of green and white spheres and all have arrows pointing from them to the ground. The first of these is labeled “Trace, superscript 14, subscript 6, C,” the second is labeled “1 percent, superscript 13, subscript 6, C” and the last is labeled “99 percent, superscript 12, subscript 6, C.” Two downward-facing arrows that merge into one arrow lead from the cow and tree to the ground and are labeled “organism dies” and “superscript 14, subscript 6, C, decay begins.” A right-facing arrow labeled on top as “Decay” and on bottom as “Time” leads from this to a label of “superscript 14, subscript 6, C, backslash, superscript 12, subscript 6, C, ratio decreased.” Near the top of the tree is a downward facing arrow with the label “superscript 14, subscript 6, C, backslash, superscript 12, subscript 6, C, ratio is constant in living organisms” that leads to the last of the lower statements.",
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "Along with stable carbon-12, radioactive carbon-14 is taken in by plants and animals, and it remains at a constant level within them while they are alive. After death, the C-14 decays and the C-14:C-12 ratio in the remains decreases. Comparing this ratio to the C-14:C-12 ratio in living organisms allows us to determine how long ago the organism lived (and died)."
                  }
                ],
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "kn34m6vvk8ow5kl",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "yxp08q8rmt0psqn",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/f9/f94721b574ad20b7cbe07090370b2619/carbon_decay.jfif",
            "type": "img",
            "width": "500"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "For example, with the half-life of "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_6^{14}\\mathrm C",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " being 5730 years, if the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_6^{14}\\mathrm C:{}_6^{12}\\mathrm C",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " ratio in a wooden object found in an archaeological dig is half what it is in a living tree, this indicates that the wooden object is 5730 years old. Highly accurate determinations of "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_6^{14}\\mathrm C:{}_6^{12}\\mathrm C",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " ratios can be obtained from very small samples (as little as a milligram) by the use of a mass spectrometer."
              }
            ],
            "id": "f9xk3rtiy00zgnk",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "id": "1063547413",
